{"id": "84baec74-2c4f-474e-83c2-f52e8d66f63d", "timestamp": "2026-10-17T19:31:03.882583", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "0d17bd5a-e709-48b8-8ddb-57b6dbd14ca4", "action": {"stage": "plan", "answer": "### Initial Proposal: Experimental Design to Evaluate MDGEN Recovery of Fast Timestep Dynamics\n\n#### 1. Proposed Approach & Experimental Pipeline\nThe core objective is to rigorously determine whether the MDGEN model can reconstruct high-frequency, sub-picosecond dynamical phenomena—such as rapid dihedral angle librations/oscillations and vibrational modes—that are aliased or entirely absent when molecular dynamics (MD) trajectories are saved at coarse subsampling intervals.\n\nWe propose a four-stage experimental workflow:\n1. **High-Frequency Reference (Ground Truth) Generation:**\n   - Simulate target systems using standard all-atom MD (e.g., OpenMM or GROMACS) with an integration step of 1.0–2.0 fs.\n   - Record coordinates, velocities, and energies at ultra-fine resolution (every 2–10 fs) to serve as exact continuous ground truth ($X_{\\text{ref}}$).\n2. **Controlled Temporal Subsampling:**\n   - Decimate the reference trajectories across multiple subsampling strides $\\Delta t_{\\text{coarse}} \\in \\{100\\text{ fs}, 500\\text{ fs}, 1\\text{ ps}, 5\\text{ ps}, 10\\text{ ps}\\}$.\n   - These subsampled series represent the sparse observation inputs ($X_{\\text{sub}}$) where fast oscillatory motions have been lost to temporal decimation.\n3. **Model Reconstruction / Super-Resolution:**\n   - Feed $X_{\\text{sub}}$ into MDGEN to predict/interpolate intermediate frames at the original fine temporal resolution ($\\hat{X}_{\\text{gen}}$).\n   - Evaluate both single-trajectory point reconstruction and ensemble/stochastic trajectory distributions (to account for the stochastic nature of Langevin/thermostatted dynamics).\n4. **Spectral and Dynamic Analysis:**\n   - Contrast reconstructed trajectories against the ground-truth high-frequency dynamics across structural, energetic, and frequency domains.\n\n---\n\n#### 2. Target Systems, Baselines, and Evaluation Metrics\n\n##### A. Target Benchmark Systems\nTo isolate dynamics across varying degrees of complexity, we propose three distinct systems:\n- **Small flexible alkane / butane:** Minimal model exhibiting clear rotational barriers and high-frequency torsional oscillations within potential wells.\n- **Alanine Dipeptide (in vacuo and explicit water):** Canonical biomolecular testbed featuring well-defined backbone dihedrals ($\\phi, \\psi$) and sidechain methyl rotations, testing both unperturbed dynamics (vacuum) and solvent-damped librations (explicit solvent).\n- **Fast-folding mini-protein (e.g., Chignolin or Trp-cage):** Multi-residue polypeptide evaluating coupled backbone/sidechain oscillations in a structured secondary motif.\n\n##### B. Baselines and Controls\n- **Positive Control:** Un-subsampled ground truth all-atom MD trajectory integrated at 2 fs.\n- **Physics-Based Baselines:**\n  - *Stochastic Brownian / Langevin Bridge:* Transition path sampling / Langevin bridge conditioned on the subsampled boundary frames.\n  - *Constrained / Restrained MD:* MD running between coarse frames with harmonic positional restraints toward the target endpoint.\n- **Geometric / Naive Baselines:**\n  - *Linear Cartesian Interpolation:* Naive midpoint interpolation (baseline showing bond-length collapse).\n  - *Internal Coordinate (Spline) Interpolation:* Smooth geometric interpolation preserving static bond lengths and angles without physical inertia.\n- **Machine Learning Baseline:** State-of-the-art SE(3)-equivariant diffusion or flow-matching trajectory interpolator (e.g., standard continuous-time diffusion model).\n\n##### C. Evaluation Metrics\n1. **Torsion Angle Oscillatory Dynamics:**\n   - **Torsional Autocorrelation Function ($C_{\\theta}(\\tau)$):** Decay rate and oscillatory frequency of $\\langle (\\theta(t) - \\bar{\\theta})(\\theta(t+\\tau) - \\bar{\\theta})\\rangle$.\n   - **Power Spectral Density (PSD) / Fourier Transform of Torsions ($\\mathcal{F}[\\theta(t)](\\omega)$):** Verifies whether peak frequencies ($10^{11} - 10^{13}\\text{ Hz}$) corresponding to torsional librations within energy wells are accurately recovered or artificially dampened/blurred.\n   - **Torsional Velocity Distribution ($p(\\dot{\\theta})$):** Tests whether the kinetic energy allocated to dihedral rotational velocity matches Maxwell-Boltzmann expectations.\n2. **Global Vibrational & Kinetic Consistency:**\n   - **Velocity Autocorrelation Function (VACF) and Vibrational Density of States (VDOS):** Fourier transform of VACF to confirm if overall vibrational spectrum matches ground truth.\n   - **Short-Time Mean Squared Displacement (MSD):** Ballistic-to-diffusive transition regime ($< 1\\text{ ps}$).\n3. **Physical & Geometric Validity:**\n   - **Bond length / bond angle RMSD from equilibrium:** Ensuring super-resolved structures do not violate covalent geometry.\n   - **Potential energy profile roughness & steric overlap violations:** Quantifying unphysical high-energy transitions during interpolation.\n\n---\n\n#### 3. Key Assumptions, Uncertainties, and Questions for Feedback\n\n1. **Model Formulation of MDGEN:**\n   - *Assumption:* MDGEN operates as a trajectory temporal super-resolution / in-betweening model (mapping coarse temporal frames to fine temporal frames), rather than an unconditioned equilibrium conformer generator.\n   - *Uncertainty:* Is MDGEN formulated strictly as a deterministic generative model, or as a stochastic generative model (e.g., score-based diffusion / flow matching) where individual sample paths represent stochastic realizations of the Langevin path?\n2. **Trajectory Conditioning & Observables:**\n   - *Assumption:* Subsampled input frames consist of Cartesian atom coordinates $(R_t)$ without recorded instantaneous velocities $(V_t)$.\n   - *Question:* Should the evaluation focus strictly on structural observables (Cartesian coordinates and derived internal angles), or should reconstructed velocities and kinetic temperatures also be evaluated?\n3. **Subsampling Regime:**\n   - *Assumption:* The critical test boundary occurs when the subsampling timestep $\\Delta t_{\\text{coarse}}$ exceeds half the period of the fastest dihedral libration (Nyquist limit, typically $\\approx 20\\text{–}100\\text{ fs}$). We plan to evaluate multiple regimes spanning sub-Nyquist to deep supra-Nyquist downsampling.", "evidence": "Inspected /workspace: directory contains only task.json and system Python 3.12 without pre-installed domain libraries. task.json confirms 'Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required', instructing the design of an experiment answering whether MDGEN recovers fast timestep dynamics in subsampled trajectories.", "assumptions": ["MDGEN is evaluated as a temporal super-resolution or trajectory interpolation model designed to fill in intermediate frames between subsampled snapshots.", "Subsampled MD inputs provide only 3D Cartesian coordinates without instantaneous velocity records.", "Evaluation must focus on fast sub-picosecond dynamics, particularly dihedral librations and vibrational modes that are lost during coarse sampling."], "uncertainties": ["Whether MDGEN is treated as a deterministic or stochastic generative model in the intended literature context.", "Whether input data to MDGEN includes solvent coordinates or only solute/biomolecule heavy atoms.", "Whether velocity/kinetic reconstruction is within scope in addition to coordinate/internal dihedral dynamics."], "kind": "ReviewHandoffAction"}, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call_407737__thought__", "tool_call": {"id": "call_407737__thought__", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"evidence\": \"Inspected /workspace: directory contains only task.json and system Python 3.12 without pre-installed domain libraries. task.json confirms 'Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required', instructing the design of an experiment answering whether MDGEN recovers fast timestep dynamics in subsampled trajectories.\", \"assumptions\": [\"MDGEN is evaluated as a temporal super-resolution or trajectory interpolation model designed to fill in intermediate frames between subsampled snapshots.\", \"Subsampled MD inputs provide only 3D Cartesian coordinates without instantaneous velocity records.\", \"Evaluation must focus on fast sub-picosecond dynamics, particularly dihedral librations and vibrational modes that are lost during coarse sampling.\"], \"answer\": \"### Initial Proposal: Experimental Design to Evaluate MDGEN Recovery of Fast Timestep Dynamics\\n\\n#### 1. Proposed Approach & Experimental Pipeline\\nThe core objective is to rigorously determine whether the MDGEN model can reconstruct high-frequency, sub-picosecond dynamical phenomena\\u2014such as rapid dihedral angle librations/oscillations and vibrational modes\\u2014that are aliased or entirely absent when molecular dynamics (MD) trajectories are saved at coarse subsampling intervals.\\n\\nWe propose a four-stage experimental workflow:\\n1. **High-Frequency Reference (Ground Truth) Generation:**\\n   - Simulate target systems using standard all-atom MD (e.g., OpenMM or GROMACS) with an integration step of 1.0\\u20132.0 fs.\\n   - Record coordinates, velocities, and energies at ultra-fine resolution (every 2\\u201310 fs) to serve as exact continuous ground truth ($X_{\\\\text{ref}}$).\\n2. **Controlled Temporal Subsampling:**\\n   - Decimate the reference trajectories across multiple subsampling strides $\\\\Delta t_{\\\\text{coarse}} \\\\in \\\\{100\\\\text{ fs}, 500\\\\text{ fs}, 1\\\\text{ ps}, 5\\\\text{ ps}, 10\\\\text{ ps}\\\\}$.\\n   - These subsampled series represent the sparse observation inputs ($X_{\\\\text{sub}}$) where fast oscillatory motions have been lost to temporal decimation.\\n3. **Model Reconstruction / Super-Resolution:**\\n   - Feed $X_{\\\\text{sub}}$ into MDGEN to predict/interpolate intermediate frames at the original fine temporal resolution ($\\\\hat{X}_{\\\\text{gen}}$).\\n   - Evaluate both single-trajectory point reconstruction and ensemble/stochastic trajectory distributions (to account for the stochastic nature of Langevin/thermostatted dynamics).\\n4. **Spectral and Dynamic Analysis:**\\n   - Contrast reconstructed trajectories against the ground-truth high-frequency dynamics across structural, energetic, and frequency domains.\\n\\n---\\n\\n#### 2. Target Systems, Baselines, and Evaluation Metrics\\n\\n##### A. Target Benchmark Systems\\nTo isolate dynamics across varying degrees of complexity, we propose three distinct systems:\\n- **Small flexible alkane / butane:** Minimal model exhibiting clear rotational barriers and high-frequency torsional oscillations within potential wells.\\n- **Alanine Dipeptide (in vacuo and explicit water):** Canonical biomolecular testbed featuring well-defined backbone dihedrals ($\\\\phi, \\\\psi$) and sidechain methyl rotations, testing both unperturbed dynamics (vacuum) and solvent-damped librations (explicit solvent).\\n- **Fast-folding mini-protein (e.g., Chignolin or Trp-cage):** Multi-residue polypeptide evaluating coupled backbone/sidechain oscillations in a structured secondary motif.\\n\\n##### B. Baselines and Controls\\n- **Positive Control:** Un-subsampled ground truth all-atom MD trajectory integrated at 2 fs.\\n- **Physics-Based Baselines:**\\n  - *Stochastic Brownian / Langevin Bridge:* Transition path sampling / Langevin bridge conditioned on the subsampled boundary frames.\\n  - *Constrained / Restrained MD:* MD running between coarse frames with harmonic positional restraints toward the target endpoint.\\n- **Geometric / Naive Baselines:**\\n  - *Linear Cartesian Interpolation:* Naive midpoint interpolation (baseline showing bond-length collapse).\\n  - *Internal Coordinate (Spline) Interpolation:* Smooth geometric interpolation preserving static bond lengths and angles without physical inertia.\\n- **Machine Learning Baseline:** State-of-the-art SE(3)-equivariant diffusion or flow-matching trajectory interpolator (e.g., standard continuous-time diffusion model).\\n\\n##### C. Evaluation Metrics\\n1. **Torsion Angle Oscillatory Dynamics:**\\n   - **Torsional Autocorrelation Function ($C_{\\\\theta}(\\\\tau)$):** Decay rate and oscillatory frequency of $\\\\langle (\\\\theta(t) - \\\\bar{\\\\theta})(\\\\theta(t+\\\\tau) - \\\\bar{\\\\theta})\\\\rangle$.\\n   - **Power Spectral Density (PSD) / Fourier Transform of Torsions ($\\\\mathcal{F}[\\\\theta(t)](\\\\omega)$):** Verifies whether peak frequencies ($10^{11} - 10^{13}\\\\text{ Hz}$) corresponding to torsional librations within energy wells are accurately recovered or artificially dampened/blurred.\\n   - **Torsional Velocity Distribution ($p(\\\\dot{\\\\theta})$):** Tests whether the kinetic energy allocated to dihedral rotational velocity matches Maxwell-Boltzmann expectations.\\n2. **Global Vibrational & Kinetic Consistency:**\\n   - **Velocity Autocorrelation Function (VACF) and Vibrational Density of States (VDOS):** Fourier transform of VACF to confirm if overall vibrational spectrum matches ground truth.\\n   - **Short-Time Mean Squared Displacement (MSD):** Ballistic-to-diffusive transition regime ($< 1\\\\text{ ps}$).\\n3. **Physical & Geometric Validity:**\\n   - **Bond length / bond angle RMSD from equilibrium:** Ensuring super-resolved structures do not violate covalent geometry.\\n   - **Potential energy profile roughness & steric overlap violations:** Quantifying unphysical high-energy transitions during interpolation.\\n\\n---\\n\\n#### 3. Key Assumptions, Uncertainties, and Questions for Feedback\\n\\n1. **Model Formulation of MDGEN:**\\n   - *Assumption:* MDGEN operates as a trajectory temporal super-resolution / in-betweening model (mapping coarse temporal frames to fine temporal frames), rather than an unconditioned equilibrium conformer generator.\\n   - *Uncertainty:* Is MDGEN formulated strictly as a deterministic generative model, or as a stochastic generative model (e.g., score-based diffusion / flow matching) where individual sample paths represent stochastic realizations of the Langevin path?\\n2. **Trajectory Conditioning & Observables:**\\n   - *Assumption:* Subsampled input frames consist of Cartesian atom coordinates $(R_t)$ without recorded instantaneous velocities $(V_t)$.\\n   - *Question:* Should the evaluation focus strictly on structural observables (Cartesian coordinates and derived internal angles), or should reconstructed velocities and kinetic temperatures also be evaluated?\\n3. **Subsampling Regime:**\\n   - *Assumption:* The critical test boundary occurs when the subsampling timestep $\\\\Delta t_{\\\\text{coarse}}$ exceeds half the period of the fastest dihedral libration (Nyquist limit, typically $\\\\approx 20\\\\text{\\u2013}100\\\\text{ fs}$). We plan to evaluate multiple regimes spanning sub-Nyquist to deep supra-Nyquist downsampling.\", \"stage\": \"plan\", \"summary\": \"Initial proposal for experimental design evaluating MDGEN recovery of fast timestep dynamics\", \"uncertainties\": [\"Whether MDGEN is treated as a deterministic or stochastic generative model in the intended literature context.\", \"Whether input data to MDGEN includes solvent coordinates or only solute/biomolecule heavy atoms.\", \"Whether velocity/kinetic reconstruction is within scope in addition to coordinate/internal dihedral dynamics.\"]}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "Ry_Uaq3PMMaapucPz-Sq4QI", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "kind": "ActionEvent", "source_event_index": 12}
