confounding factors, and criteria for determining whether MDGEN successfully recovers the missing fast dynamics.\", \"required_handoff_stage\": \"result\", \"approach_approved\": true}"}], "tool_calls": null, "tool_call_id": null, "name": null}, "llm_response_id": null, "activated_skills": [], "extended_content": [], "sender": null, "kind": "MessageEvent", "source_event_index": 14}
{"id": "c96a616b-dba1-4a23-8247-1b9143b6c2fc", "timestamp": "2026-10-17T19:32:07.214258", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "572c5714-7344-4d4f-9adb-e346f8b73a7c", "action": {"stage": "result", "answer": "# Comprehensive Experimental Design: Evaluating the Recovery of Fast Timestep Dynamics by MDGEN in Subsampled Molecular Dynamics Trajectories\n\n---\n\n## 1. Executive Summary & Research Hypothesis\n\nMolecular dynamics (MD) simulations numerically integrate Newton’s or Langevin’s equations of motion at femtosecond timesteps ($\\Delta t \\approx 1\\text{–}2\\text{ fs}$) to capture high-frequency atomic motions (covalent bond vibrations, angle bendings, and rapid dihedral librations). In standard computational practice, storing trajectories at 1–2 fs resolution is prohibitive; trajectories are routinely decimated/subsampled by factors of $10^2$ to $10^4$ (saving every 1–10 ps). This temporal decimation induces extreme information loss, violating the Nyquist-Shannon sampling theorem and extinguishing high-frequency dynamic observables.\n\n**Research Question:**  \n*Does the MDGEN generative model accurately recover the fast timestep dynamics (specifically rapid oscillations and librations in torsion angles) that are lost in subsampled molecular dynamics trajectories?*\n\n**Core Hypothesis ($H_1$):**  \nA generative trajectory super-resolution model (MDGEN) conditioned on sparse coordinate frames $R_{t_k}$ can recover the true underlying high-frequency physical dynamics if and only if the generated ensemble preserves:\n1. The characteristic high-frequency vibrational/librational peaks in the torsional Power Spectral Density (PSD) between $10^{11}$ and $10^{13}\\text{ Hz}$.\n2. The rapid oscillatory decay of the dihedral Autocorrelation Function (ACF) $C_{\\theta}(\\tau)$.\n3. Physical pseudo-velocity distributions and potential energy smoothness, without suffering from harmonic over-smoothing (mean-collapse) or unphysical high-frequency hallucination.\n\n**Null Hypothesis ($H_0$):**  \nMDGEN behaves as an effective geometric interpolator or spatial denoiser that smooths over the high-frequency librational modes (producing a low-pass filtered path) or introduces uncorrelated Gaussian white noise lacking the true physical vibrational resonances.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Mathematical Formulation of the Trajectory Recovery Task\n\n### 2.1. True and Subsampled Trajectories\nLet a molecular system consist of $N$ atoms with Cartesian coordinates $R_t \\in \\mathbb{R}^{N \\times 3}$ at time $t$.  \nThe ground-truth continuous-time trajectory integrated at fine timestep $\\delta t = 2\\text{ fs}$ over total duration $T_{tot}$ is:\n$$X_{\\text{fine}} = \\{R_0, R_{\\delta t}, R_{2\\delta t}, \\dots, R_{T_{tot}}\\}$$\n\nA subsampled trajectory with stride $K \\in \\mathbb{N}$ (coarse stride $\\Delta t = K \\cdot \\delta t$) consists of the subset of frames:\n$$X_{\\text{coarse}} = \\{R_{t_k} \\mid t_k = k \\cdot \\Delta t, \\; k = 0, 1, \\dots, M\\}, \\quad \\text{where } M = \\lfloor T_{tot} / \\Delta t \\rfloor$$\n\n### 2.2. Nyquist-Shannon Information Loss & Aliasing\nLet $\\theta(t) \\in [-\\pi, \\pi)$ denote a designated dihedral angle calculated from four atomic coordinates $(R_{t}^{(a)}, R_{t}^{(b)}, R_{t}^{(c)}, R_{t}^{(d)})$. The local motion in a torsional potential well exhibits harmonic/quasi-harmonic libration at fundamental frequency $f_{\\text{lib}} \\approx 5\\text{–}20\\text{ THz}$ (period $T_{\\text{lib}} \\approx 50\\text{–}200\\text{ fs}$).  \nBy the Nyquist-Shannon sampling theorem, continuous signal reconstruction requires a sampling rate:\n$$f_s = \\frac{1}{\\Delta t} > 2 f_{\\text{lib}} \\implies \\Delta t < \\frac{T_{\\text{lib}}}{2} \\approx 25\\text{–}100\\text{ fs}$$\nWhen $\\Delta t \\ge 500\\text{ fs}$ or $1\\text{ ps}$, the sampling rate falls deeply below the Nyquist limit ($f_s \\ll 2 f_{\\text{lib}}$). All high-frequency oscillatory dynamics are aliased or completely unobserved.\n\n### 2.3. Generative Trajectory Super-Resolution Formulation\nMDGEN is formulated as a conditional generative model (e.g., score-based continuous diffusion or conditional flow-matching) that samples the distribution over intermediate fine trajectories conditioned on boundary/subsampled frames:\n$$\\hat{X}_{\\text{fine}}^{(s)} \\sim p_\\theta(X_{\\text{fine}} \\mid X_{\\text{coarse}})$$\nwhere $s \\in \\{1, \\dots, S\\}$ indexes independent stochastic generations.  \nBecause Langevin dynamics is intrinsically stochastic due to thermal solvent collisions, recovery cannot be judged by deterministic point-to-point coordinate trajectory matching (e.g., root mean square deviation to a specific realization). Instead, recovery must be judged by **statistical and spectral ensemble equivalence** to the ground-truth Langevin distribution.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Target Molecular Systems\n\nThree target systems spanning ascending degrees of conformational and solvent complexity are established:\n\n| System | Chemical Identity | Solvation Mode | Degrees of Freedom | Target Torsional Modes |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **System A: n-Butane** | $C_4H_{10}$ (Small flexible alkane) | Vacuum / Implicit | 14 atoms | Central C-C dihedral ($\\chi_1$). Clean 1D 3-fold potential well with sharp, unhindered high-frequency librations ($f \\approx 6.5\\text{ THz}$). |\n| **System B: Alanine Dipeptide** | Ace-Ala-Nme | (1) Vacuum<br>(2) Explicit water (TIP3P, $\\sim 800$ waters) | 22 solute atoms | Backbone dihedrals $\\phi$ ($C-N-C_\\alpha-C$) and $\\psi$ ($N-C_\\alpha-C-N$); sidechain methyl $\\chi_1$. Tests coupled 2D Ramachandran librations and solvent friction damping. |\n| **System C: Chignolin** | CLN025 (10 residues: YYDPETGTWY) | Explicit water (TIP3P, $\\sim 2000$ waters, neutralized $Na^+/Cl^-$) | 166 solute atoms | Backbone turns, $\\beta$-hairpin hydrogen-bonded dihedrals, aromatic sidechain librations ($\\chi_1, \\chi_2$). Tests multi-body coupled dynamics. |\n\n---\n\n## 4. Ground-Truth All-Atom MD Simulation Protocols\n\nAll reference simulations are performed using standard MD engines (OpenMM / GROMACS) under reproducible force field and integration parameter sets:\n1. **Force Fields:**\n   - n-Butane: OPLS-AA / GAFF2.\n   - Alanine Dipeptide & Chignolin: CHARMM36m or AMBER ff19SB with OPC / TIP3P water.\n2. **Integration Parameters:**\n   - Integrator: Langevin leap-frog integrator.\n   - Timestep: $\\delta t = 1.0\\text{ fs}$ (to eliminate integration discretization artifacts up to 500 THz).\n   - Temperature: $T = 300\\text{ K}$, friction coefficient $\\gamma = 1.0\\text{ ps}^{-1}$ (or $\\gamma = 0.1\\text{ ps}^{-1}$ for vacuum to study inertial oscillations).\n   - Constraints: PME for long-range electrostatics (cutoff $1.0\\text{ nm}$); non-bonded Lennard-Jones cutoff $1.0\\text{ nm}$. Covalent bonds involving hydrogen are either unconstrained (1.0 fs step) or constrained with SHAKE/LINCS (2.0 fs step).\n3. **Trajectory Generation & Storage:**\n   - Production run per system: $100\\text{ ns}$ for Alanine Dipeptide; $200\\text{ ns}$ for Chignolin.\n   - Ground-truth save stride: every $\\delta t_{\\text{fine}} = 2.0\\text{ fs}$ ($500\\text{ frames/ps}$).\n   - Total reference frames: $5 \\times 10^7$ frames per trajectory, yielding statistically converged autocorrelation functions and Fourier spectra.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Subsampling Regimes (Relative to Torsional Libration Frequencies)\n\nBefore subsampling, the characteristic torsional oscillation frequency $f_0$ for each system is computed directly from the ground-truth reference trajectory. For alanine dipeptide in water, $\\phi$ and $\\psi$ librate in their potential wells with characteristic frequencies $f_0 \\approx 8\\text{–}15\\text{ THz}$, corresponding to an oscillation period $T_0 = 1/f_0 \\approx 65\\text{–}125\\text{ fs}$.\n\nFour systematic downsampling regimes are tested:\n1. **Regime 1: Sub-Nyquist Baseline ($\\Delta t = 20\\text{ fs}$):**\n   - $\\Delta t < T_0 / 2$. High-frequency oscillations are preserved; tests whether the model acts as an identity/interpolation filter without corrupting observable oscillations.\n2. **Regime 2: Critical Nyquist Threshold ($\\Delta t = 100\\text{ fs}$):**\n   - $\\Delta t \\approx T_0$. The sampling rate matches the boundary where direct signal recovery becomes mathematically degenerate.\n3. **Regime 3: Moderate Supra-Nyquist ($\\Delta t = 500\\text{ fs} = 0.5\\text{ ps}$):**\n   - $\\Delta t \\approx 5\\text{–}8 \\times T_0$. Torsional oscillations within wells are completely decimated; coarse frames observe only random instantaneous phases within the well.\n4. **Regime 4: Deep Supra-Nyquist / Macro-Subsampled ($\\Delta t = 5.0\\text{ ps} - 10.0\\text{ ps}$):**\n   - $\\Delta t \\gg T_0$ and $\\Delta t \\approx \\tau_{\\text{relax}}$ (comparable to the barrier-crossing or conformational relaxation timescale). Multiple transitions and hundreds of librational cycles occur between coarse frames.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Baselines and Controls\n\nTo unequivocally isolate MDGEN's contribution, the following baselines and controls are tested on identical subsampled endpoints:\n\n### 6.1. Positive Controls\n- **$C_{\\text{ref}}$ (Un-subsampled Ground Truth MD):** The exact, continuous 2 fs all-atom trajectory.\n- **$C_{\\text{Langevin-resim}}$ (Stochastic Re-simulation):** True MD re-run initialized at $R_{t_k}$ with Maxwell-Boltzmann velocity sampling, providing the physical upper bound for stochastic path divergence under the true Hamiltonian.\n\n### 6.2. Geometric and Mathematical Baselines\n- **Linear Cartesian Interpolation (LERP):**\n  $$R_{t_k + \\tau} = (1 - \\alpha) R_{t_k} + \\alpha R_{t_{k+1}}, \\quad \\alpha = \\tau / \\Delta t$$\n  *Diagnostic value:* Standard naive baseline; demonstrates steric collapse and artificial bond shortening across rotating bonds.\n- **Internal-Coordinate Spline Interpolation (IC-Spline):**\n  Conversion to Z-matrix internal coordinates $(r, \\theta, \\phi)$, natural cubic spline interpolation along dihedrals, and reconstruction to Cartesian coordinates via NeRF (Natural Extension Reference Frame).\n  *Diagnostic value:* Preserves bond lengths and angles perfectly while smoothly interpolating dihedrals; represents the optimal smooth, zero-vibration geometric path.\n\n### 6.3. Physics-Based Generative Baselines\n- **Brownian / Langevin Bridge:**\n  Harmonically steered/conditioned Langevin trajectory integrated at 2 fs between $R_{t_k}$ and $R_{t_{k+1}}$ using a time-dependent harmonic restraint:\n  $$U_{\\text{restraint}}(R, t) = \\frac{k(t)}{2} \\|R - R_{t_{k+1}}\\|^2$$\n  *Diagnostic value:* Standard physical path-sampling method driven by the true molecular mechanics force field.\n\n### 6.4. Machine Learning Baseline\n- **SE(3)-Equivariant Continuous-Time Diffusion Model:**\n  Standard score-based diffusion model conditioned on $(R_{t_k}, R_{t_{k+1}})$ without domain-specific fast-mode physics priors.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Quantitative Analysis Plan & Metrics\n\n### 7.1. Torsional Autocorrelation Function (ACF)\nFor any continuous dihedral trajectory $\\theta(t)$, compute the stationary time autocorrelation function:\n$$C_{\\theta}(\\tau) = \\frac{\\langle (\\theta(t) - \\bar{\\theta})(\\theta(t+\\tau) - \\bar{\\theta}) \\rangle_t}{\\langle (\\theta(t) - \\bar{\\theta})^2 \\rangle_t}$$\n- **True Physical Signature:** At short time lags ($\\tau < 200\\text{ fs}$), $C_{\\theta}(\\tau)$ displays underdamped or damped cosinusoidal oscillations:\n  $$C_{\\theta}(\\tau) \\approx \\cos(\\omega_0 \\tau) e^{-\\gamma \\tau / 2}$$\n- **Evaluation Metric:** \n  $$\\text{RMSE}_{\\text{ACF}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{\\tau_{\\max}} \\int_0^{\\tau_{\\max}} \\left( C_{\\theta}^{\\text{MDGEN}}(\\tau) - C_{\\theta}^{\\text{ref}}(\\tau) \\right)^2 d\\tau}, \\quad \\tau_{\\max} = 1.0\\text{ ps}$$\n\n### 7.2. Power Spectral Density (PSD) and Librational Modes\nThe frequency-domain power spectrum is computed via the Fourier transform of the windowed dihedral trajectory:\n$$S_{\\theta}(f) = \\lim_{T \\to \\infty} \\frac{1}{T} \\left| \\int_0^T \\theta(t) e^{-i 2\\pi f t} dt \\right|^2$$\nusing Welch’s average periodogram method (Hanning window of length $2\\text{ ps}$, $50\\%$ overlap).\n- **Evaluation Sub-metrics:**\n  1. **Peak Resonance Frequency Error:**\n     $$\\Delta f_{\\text{peak}} = |f_{\\text{peak}}^{\\text{MDGEN}} - f_{\\text{peak}}^{\\text{ref}}|$$\n  2. **High-Frequency Spectral Band Power Ratio ($5\\text{–}25\\text{ THz}$):**\n     $$\\mathcal{R}_{\\text{band}} = \\frac{\\int_{5\\text{ THz}}^{25\\text{ THz}} S_{\\theta}^{\\text{MDGEN}}(f) df}{\\int_{5\\text{ THz}}^{25\\text{ THz}} S_{\\theta}^{\\text{ref}}(f) df}$$\n     - $\\mathcal{R}_{\\text{band}} \\approx 1.0 \\implies$ Accurate vibrational power recovery.\n     - $\\mathcal{R}_{\\text{band}} \\ll 1.0 \\implies$ Artificial over-smoothing (loss of fast dynamics).\n     - $\\mathcal{R}_{\\text{band}} \\gg 1.0 \\implies$ High-frequency noise hallucination.\n  3. **Spectral Cosine Similarity:**\n     $$\\mathcal{S}_{\\text{cos}} = \\frac{\\int_0^{f_{\\max}} S_{\\theta}^{\\text{MDGEN}}(f) S_{\\theta}^{\\text{ref}}(f) df}{\\sqrt{\\int S_{\\theta}^{\\text{MDGEN}}(f)^2 df} \\sqrt{\\int S_{\\theta}^{\\text{ref}}(f)^2 df}}$$\n\n### 7.3. Finite-Difference Pseudo-Velocities & Vibrational Density of States (VDOS)\nBecause MDGEN outputs coordinates $R_t$, define the numerical central-difference pseudo-velocity vector:\n$$V_i(t) = \\frac{R_i(t + \\delta t) - R_i(t - \\delta t)}{2\\delta t}$$\nThe mass-weighted Velocity Autocorrelation Function (VACF) is:\n$$C_v(\\tau) = \\sum_{i=1}^N m_i \\langle V_i(t) \\cdot V_i(t+\\tau) \\rangle_t$$\nThe overall Vibrational Density of States (VDOS) is:\n$$g(f) = \\int_0^\\infty C_v(\\tau) \\cos(2\\pi f \\tau) d\\tau$$\n- **Metric:** Wasserstein-1 distance $\\mathcal{W}_1(g^{\\text{MDGEN}}, g^{\\text{ref}})$ across the vibrational spectrum ($0\\text{–}50\\text{ THz}$).\n\n### 7.4. Physical and Geometric Integrity Metrics\n1. **Bond Length and Angle RMSD:**\n   $$\\text{RMSD}_{\\text{bond}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{N_{\\text{bonds}} T} \\sum_{t} \\sum_{(i,j)} (\\|R_i(t) - R_j(t)\\| - d_{ij}^0)^2}$$\n2. **Steric Overlap Frequency:** Percentage of atom pairs with distance $r_{ij} < 0.7 \\times (\\sigma_i + \\sigma_j)$ (van der Waals overlap threshold).\n3. **Potential Energy Roughness / Distribution:**\n   Evaluate the potential energy $U(R_t)$ of MDGEN frames using the host force field; compare $p(U^{\\text{MDGEN}})$ to $p(U^{\\text{ref}})$ via Kullback-Leibler (KL) divergence.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Feasibility, Confounding Factors, and Mitigation Strategies\n\n1. **Path-Level Non-Determinism (Stochasticity) vs. Frequency Recovery:**\n   - *Confounder:* For Langevin dynamics at 300 K, two trajectories starting from identical coordinates diverge exponentially within a few hundred femtoseconds (Lyapunov time $\\approx 100\\text{ fs}$). Evaluating MDGEN via trajectory-wise coordinate RMSD against the reference will erroneously penalize physically correct stochastic paths.\n   - *Mitigation:* The primary evaluation must be strictly **distributional and spectral**. Individual realizations are evaluated via PSD, ACF decay rates, and ensemble-averaged vibrational densities over $S \\ge 50$ stochastic model generations.\n2. **Boundary Discontinuities & Conditioning Seams:**\n   - *Confounder:* Models conditioning on $R_{t_k}$ and $R_{t_{k+1}}$ may exhibit artificial kinetic \"kicks\" or velocity discontinuities at boundary knots.\n   - *Mitigation:* Explicitly compute the acceleration distribution $\\ddot{R}(t)$ across the stitched boundaries $t = t_k \\pm \\delta t$ to test for boundary seams.\n3. **Solvent Coupling and Damping:**\n   - *Confounder:* In explicit solvent, high-frequency dihedrals undergo frequent solvent collisions, damping oscillations much faster than in vacuo.\n   - *Mitigation:* Compare alanine dipeptide in both vacuum and explicit solvent. If MDGEN only captures unhindered harmonic oscillations (vacuum) but fails in explicit solvent (or vice-versa), the role of stochastic solvent friction is cleanly decoupled.\n\n---\n\n## 9. Definitive Evaluation & Decision Criteria\n\nTo provide a decisive, evidence-based answer to the research question:\n\n| Outcome Classification | Quantitative Criteria | Scientific Interpretation |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Full Accurate Recovery** | 1. Spectral band power ratio $0.85 \\le \\mathcal{R}_{\\text{band}} \\le 1.15$ in Regime 3 ($\\Delta t = 0.5\\text{–}1.0\\text{ ps}$).<br>2. Peak frequency error $\\Delta f_{\\text{peak}} < 0.5\\text{ THz}$.<br>3. $\\text{RMSE}_{\\text{ACF}} < 0.10$.<br>4. $\\text{RMSD}_{\\text{bond}} < 0.03\\text{ \\AA}$ (no unphysical distortions). | **YES.** MDGEN accurately learns the underlying physical Hamiltonian and recreates the true high-frequency librational modes lost in downsampling. |\n| **Partial / Over-Smoothed Recovery** | 1. $\\mathcal{R}_{\\text{band}} < 0.40$ (under-estimation of high-frequency power).<br>2. ACF decays monotonically without high-frequency cosinusoidal oscillations ($\\text{RMSE}_{\\text{ACF}} > 0.30$).<br>3. Geometric bonds are preserved. | **NO (Over-smoothing).** MDGEN behaves as a spatial/geometric low-pass filter (similar to spline interpolation), producing realistic static conformers but failing to recover the fast dynamic oscillations. |\n| **Artifactual / Noisy Recovery** | 1. High-frequency power present but diffuse with no resonant peak ($\\mathcal{S}_{\\text{cos}} < 0.50$).<br>2. Steric clashes $> 1\\%$ or high unphysical potential energy spikes. | **NO (Hallucination).** MDGEN injects high-frequency geometric jitter or unstructured noise rather than physical librational dynamics. |\n\n---\n\n## 10. Conclusion and Execution Blueprint\n\nThis experimental design establishes an end-to-end framework:\n- By systematically probing from sub-Nyquist ($\\Delta t = 20\\text{ fs}$) to deep supra-Nyquist ($\\Delta t = 10\\text{ ps}$) intervals across Butane, Alanine Dipeptide, and Chignolin, it isolates the exact temporal boundary where dynamic information breaks down.\n- By utilizing spectral power densities, torsional autocorrelation decays, and finite-difference VDOS alongside geometric baselines (IC-Spline) and physical baselines (Langevin bridge), it eliminates confounding factors and delivers an unambiguous answer to whether MDGEN captures true sub-picosecond molecular mechanics.", "evidence": "Exploration confirmed an isolated workspace with no existing code or datasets and system Python 3.12 without scientific packages. Track guidelines explicitly define this as a design-only task where no execution or claimed experimental execution is required. The researcher formally approved the approach and provided key clarifications: treating MDGEN as a stochastic trajectory generation model, focusing primary evaluation on coordinate-derived dihedrals and finite-difference velocities/VDOS, and analyzing solute degrees of freedom in explicit/implicit solvent.", "assumptions": ["MDGEN is formulated within the generative trajectory interpolation/super-resolution framework mapping coarse coordinate frames to fine coordinate trajectories.", "Subsampled inputs consist solely of 3D Cartesian coordinates without explicit instantaneous velocities.", "Trajectory evaluation must be performed distributionally and spectrally rather than by deterministic point-to-point coordinate matching due to intrinsic thermal/Langevin stochasticity.", "Primary fast dynamics of interest are torsional librations/oscillations and short-time vibrational density of states in the 10^11 to 10^13 Hz regime."], "uncertainties": [], "kind": "ReviewHandoffAction"}, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call_385593__thought__", "tool_call": {"id": "call_385593__thought__", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"answer\": \"# Comprehensive Experimental Design: Evaluating the Recovery of Fast Timestep Dynamics by MDGEN in Subsampled Molecular Dynamics Trajectories\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. Executive Summary & Research Hypothesis\\n\\nMolecular dynamics (MD) simulations numerically integrate Newton\\u2019s or Langevin\\u2019s equations of motion at femtosecond timesteps ($\\\\Delta t \\\\approx 1\\\\text{\\u2013}2\\\\text{ fs}$) to capture high-frequency atomic motions (covalent bond vibrations, angle bendings, and rapid dihedral librations). In standard computational practice, storing trajectories at 1\\u20132 fs resolution is prohibitive; trajectories are routinely decimated/subsampled by factors of $10^2$ to $10^4$ (saving every 1\\u201310 ps). This temporal decimation induces extreme information loss, violating the Nyquist-Shannon sampling theorem and extinguishing high-frequency dynamic observables.\\n\\n**Research Question:**  \\n*Does the MDGEN generative model accurately recover the fast timestep dynamics (specifically rapid oscillations and librations in torsion angles) that are lost in subsampled molecular dynamics trajectories?*\\n\\n**Core Hypothesis ($H_1$):**  \\nA generative trajectory super-resolution model (MDGEN) conditioned on sparse coordinate frames $R_{t_k}$ can recover the true underlying high-frequency physical dynamics if and only if the generated ensemble preserves:\\n1. The characteristic high-frequency vibrational/librational peaks in the torsional Power Spectral Density (PSD) between $10^{11}$ and $10^{13}\\\\text{ Hz}$.\\n2. The rapid oscillatory decay of the dihedral Autocorrelation Function (ACF) $C_{\\\\theta}(\\\\tau)$.\\n3. Physical pseudo-velocity distributions and potential energy smoothness, without suffering from harmonic over-smoothing (mean-collapse) or unphysical high-frequency hallucination.\\n\\n**Null Hypothesis ($H_0$):**  \\nMDGEN behaves as an effective geometric interpolator or spatial denoiser that smooths over the high-frequency librational modes (producing a low-pass filtered path) or introduces uncorrelated Gaussian white noise lacking the true physical vibrational resonances.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. Mathematical Formulation of the Trajectory Recovery Task\\n\\n### 2.1. True and Subsampled Trajectories\\nLet a molecular system consist of $N$ atoms with Cartesian coordinates $R_t \\\\in \\\\mathbb{R}^{N \\\\times 3}$ at time $t$.  \\nThe ground-truth continuous-time trajectory integrated at fine timestep $\\\\delta t = 2\\\\text{ fs}$ over total duration $T_{tot}$ is:\\n$$X_{\\\\text{fine}} = \\\\{R_0, R_{\\\\delta t}, R_{2\\\\delta t}, \\\\dots, R_{T_{tot}}\\\\}$$\\n\\nA subsampled trajectory with stride $K \\\\in \\\\mathbb{N}$ (coarse stride $\\\\Delta t = K \\\\cdot \\\\delta t$) consists of the subset of frames:\\n$$X_{\\\\text{coarse}} = \\\\{R_{t_k} \\\\mid t_k = k \\\\cdot \\\\Delta t, \\\\; k = 0, 1, \\\\dots, M\\\\}, \\\\quad \\\\text{where } M = \\\\lfloor T_{tot} / \\\\Delta t \\\\rfloor$$\\n\\n### 2.2. Nyquist-Shannon Information Loss & Aliasing\\nLet $\\\\theta(t) \\\\in [-\\\\pi, \\\\pi)$ denote a designated dihedral angle calculated from four atomic coordinates $(R_{t}^{(a)}, R_{t}^{(b)}, R_{t}^{(c)}, R_{t}^{(d)})$. The local motion in a torsional potential well exhibits harmonic/quasi-harmonic libration at fundamental frequency $f_{\\\\text{lib}} \\\\approx 5\\\\text{\\u2013}20\\\\text{ THz}$ (period $T_{\\\\text{lib}} \\\\approx 50\\\\text{\\u2013}200\\\\text{ fs}$).  \\nBy the Nyquist-Shannon sampling theorem, continuous signal reconstruction requires a sampling rate:\\n$$f_s = \\\\frac{1}{\\\\Delta t} > 2 f_{\\\\text{lib}} \\\\implies \\\\Delta t < \\\\frac{T_{\\\\text{lib}}}{2} \\\\approx 25\\\\text{\\u2013}100\\\\text{ fs}$$\\nWhen $\\\\Delta t \\\\ge 500\\\\text{ fs}$ or $1\\\\text{ ps}$, the sampling rate falls deeply below the Nyquist limit ($f_s \\\\ll 2 f_{\\\\text{lib}}$). All high-frequency oscillatory dynamics are aliased or completely unobserved.\\n\\n### 2.3. Generative Trajectory Super-Resolution Formulation\\nMDGEN is formulated as a conditional generative model (e.g., score-based continuous diffusion or conditional flow-matching) that samples the distribution over intermediate fine trajectories conditioned on boundary/subsampled frames:\\n$$\\\\hat{X}_{\\\\text{fine}}^{(s)} \\\\sim p_\\\\theta(X_{\\\\text{fine}} \\\\mid X_{\\\\text{coarse}})$$\\nwhere $s \\\\in \\\\{1, \\\\dots, S\\\\}$ indexes independent stochastic generations.  \\nBecause Langevin dynamics is intrinsically stochastic due to thermal solvent collisions, recovery cannot be judged by deterministic point-to-point coordinate trajectory matching (e.g., root mean square deviation to a specific realization). Instead, recovery must be judged by **statistical and spectral ensemble equivalence** to the ground-truth Langevin distribution.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. Target Molecular Systems\\n\\nThree target systems spanning ascending degrees of conformational and solvent complexity are established:\\n\\n| System | Chemical Identity | Solvation Mode | Degrees of Freedom | Target Torsional Modes |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **System A: n-Butane** | $C_4H_{10}$ (Small flexible alkane) | Vacuum / Implicit | 14 atoms | Central C-C dihedral ($\\\\chi_1$). Clean 1D 3-fold potential well with sharp, unhindered high-frequency librations ($f \\\\approx 6.5\\\\text{ THz}$). |\\n| **System B: Alanine Dipeptide** | Ace-Ala-Nme | (1) Vacuum<br>(2) Explicit water (TIP3P, $\\\\sim 800$ waters) | 22 solute atoms | Backbone dihedrals $\\\\phi$ ($C-N-C_\\\\alpha-C$) and $\\\\psi$ ($N-C_\\\\alpha-C-N$); sidechain methyl $\\\\chi_1$. Tests coupled 2D Ramachandran librations and solvent friction damping. |\\n| **System C: Chignolin** | CLN025 (10 residues: YYDPETGTWY) | Explicit water (TIP3P, $\\\\sim 2000$ waters, neutralized $Na^+/Cl^-$) | 166 solute atoms | Backbone turns, $\\\\beta$-hairpin hydrogen-bonded dihedrals, aromatic sidechain librations ($\\\\chi_1, \\\\chi_2$). Tests multi-body coupled dynamics. |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 4. Ground-Truth All-Atom MD Simulation Protocols\\n\\nAll reference simulations are performed using standard MD engines (OpenMM / GROMACS) under reproducible force field and integration parameter sets:\\n1. **Force Fields:**\\n   - n-Butane: OPLS-AA / GAFF2.\\n   - Alanine Dipeptide & Chignolin: CHARMM36m or AMBER ff19SB with OPC / TIP3P water.\\n2. **Integration Parameters:**\\n   - Integrator: Langevin leap-frog integrator.\\n   - Timestep: $\\\\delta t = 1.0\\\\text{ fs}$ (to eliminate integration discretization artifacts up to 500 THz).\\n   - Temperature: $T = 300\\\\text{ K}$, friction coefficient $\\\\gamma = 1.0\\\\text{ ps}^{-1}$ (or $\\\\gamma = 0.1\\\\text{ ps}^{-1}$ for vacuum to study inertial oscillations).\\n   - Constraints: PME for long-range electrostatics (cutoff $1.0\\\\text{ nm}$); non-bonded Lennard-Jones cutoff $1.0\\\\text{ nm}$. Covalent bonds involving hydrogen are either unconstrained (1.0 fs step) or constrained with SHAKE/LINCS (2.0 fs step).\\n3. **Trajectory Generation & Storage:**\\n   - Production run per system: $100\\\\text{ ns}$ for Alanine Dipeptide; $200\\\\text{ ns}$ for Chignolin.\\n   - Ground-truth save stride: every $\\\\delta t_{\\\\text{fine}} = 2.0\\\\text{ fs}$ ($500\\\\text{ frames/ps}$).\\n   - Total reference frames: $5 \\\\times 10^7$ frames per trajectory, yielding statistically converged autocorrelation functions and Fourier spectra.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 5. Subsampling Regimes (Relative to Torsional Libration Frequencies)\\n\\nBefore subsampling, the characteristic torsional oscillation frequency $f_0$ for each system is computed directly from the ground-truth reference trajectory. For alanine dipeptide in water, $\\\\phi$ and $\\\\psi$ librate in their potential wells with characteristic frequencies $f_0 \\\\approx 8\\\\text{\\u2013}15\\\\text{ THz}$, corresponding to an oscillation period $T_0 = 1/f_0 \\\\approx 65\\\\text{\\u2013}125\\\\text{ fs}$.\\n\\nFour systematic downsampling regimes are tested:\\n1. **Regime 1: Sub-Nyquist Baseline ($\\\\Delta t = 20\\\\text{ fs}$):**\\n   - $\\\\Delta t < T_0 / 2$. High-frequency oscillations are preserved; tests whether the model acts as an identity/interpolation filter without corrupting observable oscillations.\\n2. **Regime 2: Critical Nyquist Threshold ($\\\\Delta t = 100\\\\text{ fs}$):**\\n   - $\\\\Delta t \\\\approx T_0$. The sampling rate matches the boundary where direct signal recovery becomes mathematically degenerate.\\n3. **Regime 3: Moderate Supra-Nyquist ($\\\\Delta t = 500\\\\text{ fs} = 0.5\\\\text{ ps}$):**\\n   - $\\\\Delta t \\\\approx 5\\\\text{\\u2013}8 \\\\times T_0$. Torsional oscillations within wells are completely decimated; coarse frames observe only random instantaneous phases within the well.\\n4. **Regime 4: Deep Supra-Nyquist / Macro-Subsampled ($\\\\Delta t = 5.0\\\\text{ ps} - 10.0\\\\text{ ps}$):**\\n   - $\\\\Delta t \\\\gg T_0$ and $\\\\Delta t \\\\approx \\\\tau_{\\\\text{relax}}$ (comparable to the barrier-crossing or conformational relaxation timescale). Multiple transitions and hundreds of librational cycles occur between coarse frames.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 6. Baselines and Controls\\n\\nTo unequivocally isolate MDGEN's contribution, the following baselines and controls are tested on identical subsampled endpoints:\\n\\n### 6.1. Positive Controls\\n- **$C_{\\\\text{ref}}$ (Un-subsampled Ground Truth MD):** The exact, continuous 2 fs all-atom trajectory.\\n- **$C_{\\\\text{Langevin-resim}}$ (Stochastic Re-simulation):** True MD re-run initialized at $R_{t_k}$ with Maxwell-Boltzmann velocity sampling, providing the physical upper bound for stochastic path divergence under the true Hamiltonian.\\n\\n### 6.2. Geometric and Mathematical Baselines\\n- **Linear Cartesian Interpolation (LERP):**\\n  $$R_{t_k + \\\\tau} = (1 - \\\\alpha) R_{t_k} + \\\\alpha R_{t_{k+1}}, \\\\quad \\\\alpha = \\\\tau / \\\\Delta t$$\\n  *Diagnostic value:* Standard naive baseline; demonstrates steric collapse and artificial bond shortening across rotating bonds.\\n- **Internal-Coordinate Spline Interpolation (IC-Spline):**\\n  Conversion to Z-matrix internal coordinates $(r, \\\\theta, \\\\phi)$, natural cubic spline interpolation along dihedrals, and reconstruction to Cartesian coordinates via NeRF (Natural Extension Reference Frame).\\n  *Diagnostic value:* Preserves bond lengths and angles perfectly while smoothly interpolating dihedrals; represents the optimal smooth, zero-vibration geometric path.\\n\\n### 6.3. Physics-Based Generative Baselines\\n- **Brownian / Langevin Bridge:**\\n  Harmonically steered/conditioned Langevin trajectory integrated at 2 fs between $R_{t_k}$ and $R_{t_{k+1}}$ using a time-dependent harmonic restraint:\\n  $$U_{\\\\text{restraint}}(R, t) = \\\\frac{k(t)}{2} \\\\|R - R_{t_{k+1}}\\\\|^2$$\\n  *Diagnostic value:* Standard physical path-sampling method driven by the true molecular mechanics force field.\\n\\n### 6.4. Machine Learning Baseline\\n- **SE(3)-Equivariant Continuous-Time Diffusion Model:**\\n  Standard score-based diffusion model conditioned on $(R_{t_k}, R_{t_{k+1}})$ without domain-specific fast-mode physics priors.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 7. Quantitative Analysis Plan & Metrics\\n\\n### 7.1. Torsional Autocorrelation Function (ACF)\\nFor any continuous dihedral trajectory $\\\\theta(t)$, compute the stationary time autocorrelation function:\\n$$C_{\\\\theta}(\\\\tau) = \\\\frac{\\\\langle (\\\\theta(t) - \\\\bar{\\\\theta})(\\\\theta(t+\\\\tau) - \\\\bar{\\\\theta}) \\\\rangle_t}{\\\\langle (\\\\theta(t) - \\\\bar{\\\\theta})^2 \\\\rangle_t}$$\\n- **True Physical Signature:** At short time lags ($\\\\tau < 200\\\\text{ fs}$), $C_{\\\\theta}(\\\\tau)$ displays underdamped or damped cosinusoidal oscillations:\\n  $$C_{\\\\theta}(\\\\tau) \\\\approx \\\\cos(\\\\omega_0 \\\\tau) e^{-\\\\gamma \\\\tau / 2}$$\\n- **Evaluation Metric:** \\n  $$\\\\text{RMSE}_{\\\\text{ACF}} = \\\\sqrt{\\\\frac{1}{\\\\tau_{\\\\max}} \\\\int_0^{\\\\tau_{\\\\max}} \\\\left( C_{\\\\theta}^{\\\\text{MDGEN}}(\\\\tau) - C_{\\\\theta}^{\\\\text{ref}}(\\\\tau) \\\\right)^2 d\\\\tau}, \\\\quad \\\\tau_{\\\\max} = 1.0\\\\text{ ps}$$\\n\\n### 7.2. Power Spectral Density (PSD) and Librational Modes\\nThe frequency-domain power spectrum is computed via the Fourier transform of the windowed dihedral trajectory:\\n$$S_{\\\\theta}(f) = \\\\lim_{T \\\\to \\\\infty} \\\\frac{1}{T} \\\\left| \\\\int_0^T \\\\theta(t) e^{-i 2\\\\pi f t} dt \\\\right|^2$$\\nusing Welch\\u2019s average periodogram method (Hanning window of length $2\\\\text{ ps}$, $50\\\\%$ overlap).\\n- **Evaluation Sub-metrics:**\\n  1. **Peak Resonance Frequency Error:**\\n     $$\\\\Delta f_{\\\\text{peak}} = |f_{\\\\text{peak}}^{\\\\text{MDGEN}} - f_{\\\\text{peak}}^{\\\\text{ref}}|$$\\n  2. **High-Frequency Spectral Band Power Ratio ($5\\\\text{\\u2013}25\\\\text{ THz}$):**\\n     $$\\\\mathcal{R}_{\\\\text{band}} = \\\\frac{\\\\int_{5\\\\text{ THz}}^{25\\\\text{ THz}} S_{\\\\theta}^{\\\\text{MDGEN}}(f) df}{\\\\int_{5\\\\text{ THz}}^{25\\\\text{ THz}} S_{\\\\theta}^{\\\\text{ref}}(f) df}$$\\n     - $\\\\mathcal{R}_{\\\\text{band}} \\\\approx 1.0 \\\\implies$ Accurate vibrational power recovery.\\n     - $\\\\mathcal{R}_{\\\\text{band}} \\\\ll 1.0 \\\\implies$ Artificial over-smoothing (loss of fast dynamics).\\n     - $\\\\mathcal{R}_{\\\\text{band}} \\\\gg 1.0 \\\\implies$ High-frequency noise hallucination.\\n  3. **Spectral Cosine Similarity:**\\n     $$\\\\mathcal{S}_{\\\\text{cos}} = \\\\frac{\\\\int_0^{f_{\\\\max}} S_{\\\\theta}^{\\\\text{MDGEN}}(f) S_{\\\\theta}^{\\\\text{ref}}(f) df}{\\\\sqrt{\\\\int S_{\\\\theta}^{\\\\text{MDGEN}}(f)^2 df} \\\\sqrt{\\\\int S_{\\\\theta}^{\\\\text{ref}}(f)^2 df}}$$\\n\\n### 7.3. Finite-Difference Pseudo-Velocities & Vibrational Density of States (VDOS)\\nBecause MDGEN outputs coordinates $R_t$, define the numerical central-difference pseudo-velocity vector:\\n$$V_i(t) = \\\\frac{R_i(t + \\\\delta t) - R_i(t - \\\\delta t)}{2\\\\delta t}$$\\nThe mass-weighted Velocity Autocorrelation Function (VACF) is:\\n$$C_v(\\\\tau) = \\\\sum_{i=1}^N m_i \\\\langle V_i(t) \\\\cdot V_i(t+\\\\tau) \\\\rangle_t$$\\nThe overall Vibrational Density of States (VDOS) is:\\n$$g(f) = \\\\int_0^\\\\infty C_v(\\\\tau) \\\\cos(2\\\\pi f \\\\tau) d\\\\tau$$\\n- **Metric:** Wasserstein-1 distance $\\\\mathcal{W}_1(g^{\\\\text{MDGEN}}, g^{\\\\text{ref}})$ across the vibrational spectrum ($0\\\\text{\\u2013}50\\\\text{ THz}$).\\n\\n### 7.4. Physical and Geometric Integrity Metrics\\n1. **Bond Length and Angle RMSD:**\\n   $$\\\\text{RMSD}_{\\\\text{bond}} = \\\\sqrt{\\\\frac{1}{N_{\\\\text{bonds}} T} \\\\sum_{t} \\\\sum_{(i,j)} (\\\\|R_i(t) - R_j(t)\\\\| - d_{ij}^0)^2}$$\\n2. **Steric Overlap Frequency:** Percentage of atom pairs with distance $r_{ij} < 0.7 \\\\times (\\\\sigma_i + \\\\sigma_j)$ (van der Waals overlap threshold).\\n3. **Potential Energy Roughness / Distribution:**\\n   Evaluate the potential energy $U(R_t)$ of MDGEN frames using the host force field; compare $p(U^{\\\\text{MDGEN}})$ to $p(U^{\\\\text{ref}})$ via Kullback-Leibler (KL) divergence.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 8. Feasibility, Confounding Factors, and Mitigation Strategies\\n\\n1. **Path-Level Non-Determinism (Stochasticity) vs. Frequency Recovery:**\\n   - *Confounder:* For Langevin dynamics at 300 K, two trajectories starting from identical coordinates diverge exponentially within a few hundred femtoseconds (Lyapunov time $\\\\approx 100\\\\text{ fs}$). Evaluating MDGEN via trajectory-wise coordinate RMSD against the reference will erroneously penalize physically correct stochastic paths.\\n   - *Mitigation:* The primary evaluation must be strictly **distributional and spectral**. Individual realizations are evaluated via PSD, ACF decay rates, and ensemble-averaged vibrational densities over $S \\\\ge 50$ stochastic model generations.\\n2. **Boundary Discontinuities & Conditioning Seams:**\\n   - *Confounder:* Models conditioning on $R_{t_k}$ and $R_{t_{k+1}}$ may exhibit artificial kinetic \\\"kicks\\\" or velocity discontinuities at boundary knots.\\n   - *Mitigation:* Explicitly compute the acceleration distribution $\\\\ddot{R}(t)$ across the stitched boundaries $t = t_k \\\\pm \\\\delta t$ to test for boundary seams.\\n3. **Solvent Coupling and Damping:**\\n   - *Confounder:* In explicit solvent, high-frequency dihedrals undergo frequent solvent collisions, damping oscillations much faster than in vacuo.\\n   - *Mitigation:* Compare alanine dipeptide in both vacuum and explicit solvent. If MDGEN only captures unhindered harmonic oscillations (vacuum) but fails in explicit solvent (or vice-versa), the role of stochastic solvent friction is cleanly decoupled.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 9. Definitive Evaluation & Decision Criteria\\n\\nTo provide a decisive, evidence-based answer to the research question:\\n\\n| Outcome Classification | Quantitative Criteria | Scientific Interpretation |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Full Accurate Recovery** | 1. Spectral band power ratio $0.85 \\\\le \\\\mathcal{R}_{\\\\text{band}} \\\\le 1.15$ in Regime 3 ($\\\\Delta t = 0.5\\\\text{\\u2013}1.0\\\\text{ ps}$).<br>2. Peak frequency error $\\\\Delta f_{\\\\text{peak}} < 0.5\\\\text{ THz}$.<br>3. $\\\\text{RMSE}_{\\\\text{ACF}} < 0.10$.<br>4. $\\\\text{RMSD}_{\\\\text{bond}} < 0.03\\\\text{ \\\\AA}$ (no unphysical distortions). | **YES.** MDGEN accurately learns the underlying physical Hamiltonian and recreates the true high-frequency librational modes lost in downsampling. |\\n| **Partial / Over-Smoothed Recovery** | 1. $\\\\mathcal{R}_{\\\\text{band}} < 0.40$ (under-estimation of high-frequency power).<br>2. ACF decays monotonically without high-frequency cosinusoidal oscillations ($\\\\text{RMSE}_{\\\\text{ACF}} > 0.30$).<br>3. Geometric bonds are preserved. | **NO (Over-smoothing).** MDGEN behaves as a spatial/geometric low-pass filter (similar to spline interpolation), producing realistic static conformers but failing to recover the fast dynamic oscillations. |\\n| **Artifactual / Noisy Recovery** | 1. High-frequency power present but diffuse with no resonant peak ($\\\\mathcal{S}_{\\\\text{cos}} < 0.50$).<br>2. Steric clashes $> 1\\\\%$ or high unphysical potential energy spikes. | **NO (Hallucination).** MDGEN injects high-frequency geometric jitter or unstructured noise rather than physical librational dynamics. |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 10. Conclusion and Execution Blueprint\\n\\nThis experimental design establishes an end-to-end framework:\\n- By systematically probing from sub-Nyquist ($\\\\Delta t = 20\\\\text{ fs}$) to deep supra-Nyquist ($\\\\Delta t = 10\\\\text{ ps}$) intervals across Butane, Alanine Dipeptide, and Chignolin, it isolates the exact temporal boundary where dynamic information breaks down.\\n- By utilizing spectral power densities, torsional autocorrelation decays, and finite-difference VDOS alongside geometric baselines (IC-Spline) and physical baselines (Langevin bridge), it eliminates confounding factors and delivers an unambiguous answer to whether MDGEN captures true sub-picosecond molecular mechanics.\", \"stage\": \"result\", \"assumptions\": [\"MDGEN is formulated within the generative trajectory interpolation/super-resolution framework mapping coarse coordinate frames to fine coordinate trajectories.\", \"Subsampled inputs consist solely of 3D Cartesian coordinates without explicit instantaneous velocities.\", \"Trajectory evaluation must be performed distributionally and spectrally rather than by deterministic point-to-point coordinate matching due to intrinsic thermal/Langevin stochasticity.\", \"Primary fast dynamics of interest are torsional librations/oscillations and short-time vibrational density of states in the 10^11 to 10^13 Hz regime.\"], \"uncertainties\": [], \"evidence\": \"Exploration confirmed an isolated workspace with no existing code or datasets and system Python 3.12 without scientific packages. Track guidelines explicitly define this as a design-only task where no execution or claimed experimental execution is required. The researcher formally approved the approach and provided key clarifications: treating MDGEN as a stochastic trajectory generation model, focusing primary evaluation on coordinate-derived dihedrals and finite-difference velocities/VDOS, and analyzing solute degrees of freedom in explicit/implicit solvent.\", \"summary\": \"Complete, rigorous experimental design deliverable evaluating MDGEN recovery of fast timestep dynamics in subsampled MD trajectories\"}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "dC_Uaq61INeW9LsP6KbXkQY", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "kind": "ActionEvent", "source_event_index": 15}
